TASK A3 – DESIGN WITH ABSTRACT CLASSES (5 Marks)
A3.1 – Payer Interface (3 Marks)
An abstract class Payer is designed with a pure virtual function amountCovered(const Bill&) to calculate the amount covered for a bill.

Derived classes:
1. SelfPay – The patient pays the entire bill amount.
2. Insurance – Covers the bill based on the cover limit and co-payment percentage.
3. GovernmentScheme – Covers the bill according to fixed package rates.
Abstract Class Design
class Payer {
public:
    virtual double amountCovered(const Bill& bill) const = 0;
    virtual ~Payer() = default;
};

class SelfPay : public Payer {
public:
    double amountCovered(const Bill& bill) const override;
};

class Insurance : public Payer {
private:
    double coverLimit;
    double coPaymentPercentage;

public:
    double amountCovered(const Bill& bill) const override;
};

class GovernmentScheme : public Payer {
public:
    double amountCovered(const Bill& bill) const override;
};


Working:
- SelfPay → Patient pays 100% of the bill.
- Insurance → Insurance pays according to the cover limit and co-payment percentage.
- GovernmentScheme → Coverage is calculated using fixed package rates.

A3.2 – Extension (2 Marks)
A new payer, such as CorporateTieUp, can be added by creating a new derived class from Payer and overriding amountCovered().
class CorporateTieUp : public Payer {
public:
    double amountCovered(const Bill& bill) const override {
        return bill.total() * 0.80;
    }
};


This payer covers 80% of the bill amount.

The existing Payer, SelfPay, Insurance and GovernmentScheme classes do not need to be modified. This follows the Open-Closed Principle, which states that software should be open for extension but closed for modification.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Instead of using abstract classes, payer policies can be implemented using function pointers or std::function.

Example:
double selfPay(const Bill& b) {
    return b.total();
}

double insurancePay(const Bill& b) {
    return b.total() * 0.80;
}

double governmentPay(const Bill& b) {
    return b.total() * 0.60;
}


Feature	Abstract Payer Class	Function Pointer / std::function
Time per call	O(1)	O(1)
Space	Object data + vtable pointer	Function object/pointer + stored data
Cover limit storage	Stored in derived class	Stored separately or captured in function
Extension	Add a new derived class	Add a new policy function
Maintainability	Structured and easy to extend	Flexible but may become less organized

Conclusion: The abstract Payer class is more suitable for the hospital billing system because it provides a common interface, supports runtime polymorphism and allows new payer types to be added without modifying existing classes.
